{-
   The one condition a qualified type argument has that is not about structure.

   # resolver: ownership

   `Pair`'s second field is a `Guard` this value owns, so something still dies with it - and a
   derived teardown is generated per *type*, which is the record's own field list with no way to be
   told that one of its positions belongs to somebody else. So the qualification is admitted only
   where the removal leaves nothing to release and nothing to deep copy, which is every wrapper whose
   members are exactly its parameters and is not this one.

   In a file of its own because the check runs after `classifyTypes` - what a `Pair` releases is a
   question about the whole program - and a file with any declaration error never gets that far.
   `Reject.Argument.yana` is the structural half.
-}

data Guard {id: I32}

instance Drop(Guard):
    fn drop(->value: Guard) -> {} = {}

data Pair(k, v) {key: k, value: v}

fn residue(g: a'Guard, ->own: Guard) -> Pair(a'Guard, Guard) = Pair {key: g, value: own}

fn main() -> I32 = 0
